          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"alcott-little-women-cb042560b1b039fd\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Laurie directly avows a loving state that has persisted throughout his acquaintance with Jo, making T dominant. The avowal also participates in a deliberate courtship bid demanding an answer, which gives it moderate P support, though the undertaking force is carried more explicitly by the surrounding demand than by “I’ve loved you” itself. His agitation and impetuous delivery do not establish an independently reflexive or discharge-like E force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"P is moderately supported because the avowal functions within a proposal-like attempt to change their relationship, but the highlighted clause itself primarily states an enduring emotional condition.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Laurie speaks the avowal directly to Jo during an intentional face-to-face declaration of love.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Laurie is earnest and emotionally agitated, but the confession is anticipated and deliberately initiated: he insists that Jo hear him and give an answer. It serves as evidence and justification for his courtship bid. Jo has foreseen the declaration, does not reciprocate the desired kind of love, and explicitly refuses to lie about her feelings. The choking voice and impetuosity intensify the scene without making the highlighted avowal independently expressive/reflexive.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’ve loved you ever since I’ve known you, Jo”\",\"supports\":\"Strong T: Laurie explicitly presents his enduring love for Jo as true.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Now I’m going to make you hear, and give me an answer, for I can’t go on so any longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Moderate P: the avowal is deployed as part of a deliberate courtship bid seeking a relational answer.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Laurie “meant to ‘have it out’” and spoke with characteristic impetuosity while trying to keep his intermittently choking voice steady.\",\"supports\":\"The event is emotionally intense but planned and proposition-directed; this favors passionate T/P delivery rather than independent E force.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jo answers that she cannot love him “as you want me to” and that “it would be a lie to say I do when I don’t.”\",\"supports\":\"The participants treat love language primarily as an avowal whose truth depends on the speaker’s emotional state, reinforcing T.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified in Chapter Thirty-Five of Louisa May Alcott’s Little Women as an affirmative perfect-form explicit love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the textual location and the perfect-aspect framing of a continuing prior state.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the explicit avowal, while moderate P captures its role in a courtship undertaking. Emotional delivery is contextual rather than an independently necessary E force, and no residual core function requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
